questionText:
Given the real class-21 code:

public class YouTubeChannel implements ISubject {
    private List<ISubscriber> subscribers;
    private void notifySubscribers(String message) {
        for (ISubscriber subscriber : subscribers) {
            subscriber.notifyUser(message);
        }
    }
}

Why does this loop never need an if-else on subscriber type to decide how to notify each one?

EmailSubscriber.java (real course code):

public class EmailSubscriber extends User {
    private String email;
    @Override
    public void notifyUser(String message) {
        System.out.println("Email sending started to " + email);
        super.notifyUser(message);
        System.out.println("Email sending completed");
    }
}

User.notifyUser() prints "notification " + message. What is the exact console output when this subscriber is notified with message "upload completed on X"?

Given SMSSubscriber's constructor:

public SMSSubscriber(String phoneNumber) {
    this.phoneNumber = phoneNumber;
}

and that it extends User (which has a private String name field with no matching constructor param here), what is kotari.getName() equal to immediately after new SMSSubscriber("9191919191") runs?

In the real ISubject interface:

public interface ISubject {
    void subscribe(ISubscriber subscriber);
    void unsubscribe(ISubscriber subscriber);
}

notice there's no notify(...) method declared here at all — notifySubscribers() only exists inside YouTubeChannel as a private helper. What does this tell you about what ISubject actually guarantees to any class that implements it?

Given YouTubeChannel's constructor:

public YouTubeChannel(String channel) {
    this.channel = channel;
    subscribers = new ArrayList<>();
}

why is subscribers initialized inside the constructor rather than at the field declaration (private List<ISubscriber> subscribers = new ArrayList<>();), and does it matter functionally here?
